 a lot less as filming  her piloting scenes actually made her  sick on set number 15. when you're the  master of the Mystic Arts you need to  know how to fly Benedict Cumberbatch  already had plenty of experience with  harnesses before he'd even joined the  MCU as Steven strange a photo set of him  on the set of Sherlock shows Cumberbatch  hanging from a harness off the side of a  building comfortably enough to take off  his jacket and smile in Doctor Strange  tuning Forks are also used for more  three-dimensional aerial movement number  16 Henry Cavill takes his stunt work  seriously the flight for Man of Steel  was accomplished in a number of ways  from wire rigging to gimbal rigs CGI  helped enhance some of the more  cinematic flight scenes but seeing as  Henry Cavill believes in doing as many  of his own stunts as possible it comes  as no surprise that he was willing to  strap himself into a harness whenever he  could number 17 filming for television  in the 1970s sure was a different scene  for Wonder Woman Linda Carter once  dangled mid-air from a helicopter  without the use of wires wrist guards or  safety nets Carter's stunt double was  supposed to step in for her but she  decided to do it herself when it was  discovered that the stunt double didn't  look enough like her Carter got in  trouble with the network afterwards for  the unnecessary risk but otherwise  walked away unscathed number 18 you  could not pay us enough to try this  stunt for his role as Hancock Will Smith  was not only suspended 200 feet above  the city landscape on wires but the  crane would actually proceed to propel  him at 50 miles per hour Believe It or  Not Smith was complete completely cool  with the idea stating that the computer  effects look great but it's more fun to  get your hands dirty number 19 flying  isn't quite as easy as Faith trust and  pixie dust the 2003 movie Peter Pan used  both wires for the more intensive flying  stunts as well as tuning forks for more  Dynamic aerial shots Jeremy Sumpter said  the experience was amazing but  emphasized how much work it involved  claiming that he had to exercise for two  hours a day six days a week in order to  perform the stunts number 20 it helps to  have some experience the 1978 Superman  film pioneered the use of the zoptic  front projection system a camera effect  that would allow Christopher Reeve to  appear as though he were flying even if  he were lying completely flat using his  prior experience as a trained pilot  Reeves simply imagined that he were in  Flight while being suspended from the  wires so that he would actually move his  arms as though he were sitting in a  plane although these actors were asked  to perform superhuman Feats for the sake  of of a role they still manage to make  it look easy which flying scenes were  you most impressed by
 

